17] [17592186050282 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050282 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050282 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050282 #edb/ref 1031 "Unit Conversions Using Base Units of Mass" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050282 #edb/ref 1033 3868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050282 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3c94ddf9-ba20-52cc-bd59-5aeaadb59184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1031 "Converting Between Mixed Units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1033 2233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "42e7a396-f7f5-5ef7-82f9-ae4f6cfc0ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051042 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051042 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051042 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051042 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051042 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051042 #edb/ref 1031 "Unit Conversions Between Mixed Units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051042 #edb/ref 1033 6634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051042 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7cd09b39-0984-59e4-803e-e75a1365d0ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135916 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0d620be5-872f-567c-b4eb-13ab7a596c28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138314 #edb/ref 1069 "e-907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138314 #edb/ref 1071 "A guitar string, made of carbon steel, has a linear mass density of $3\\,950$ milligrams per meter. What is the linear mass density in grams per meter?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138314 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "638af342-1995-5403-84bd-b6c720cb432d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138314 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{mg}$ with $\\textrm{g}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{mg},$ we can put $\\textrm{mg}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{g},$ we can put $\\textrm{g}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{mg}} }{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{\\cancel{ \\textrm{mg} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg} }{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\textrm{mg} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The density given in the problem statement is $3\\,950 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ milligrams per gram. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$ grams per milligram.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,950\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{m}} & =3\\,950\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mg}} \\\\  & =(3\\,950\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000})\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mg}}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mg}}} \\\\  & =3.95\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{m}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the linear mass density of the string is $3.95\\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138315 #edb/ref 1069 "e-908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138315 #edb/ref 1071 "Sand runs through a sand timer at a rate of $120$ grams per minute. What is this rate in grams per second?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138315 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08aa3547-cb51-5d00-8cb7-3e1a9bd4a695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138315 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{min}$ with $\\textrm{s}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{min},$ we can put $\\textrm{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{s},$ we can put $\\textrm{s}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{ \\cancel{ \\textrm{min} } }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\textrm{min}}\\cdot \\dfrac{  \\textrm{min}  }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $120 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}} & =120\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =(120\\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the sand runs through the sand timer at a rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138316 #edb/ref 1069 "e-909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138316 #edb/ref 1071 "A peregrine falcon can reach a speed of $360$ kilometers per hour during flight while diving. What is this speed in meters per second?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138316 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79f9c66b-2e4e-53ad-8146-10a2aa4e730d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138316 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}} }{\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}} } \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{hour}$ into $\\textrm{s}.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}} }{ \\cancel{\\color{red}\\textrm{hour}} }\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}} } \\cdot \\dfrac{ \\cancel{\\color{red}\\textrm{hour}} }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{black}\\textrm{km} }{ \\color{black}\\textrm{hour} }\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\color{black}\\textrm{km} } \\cdot \\dfrac{ \\color{black}\\textrm{hour} }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $360 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n\n- The number of seconds in one hour is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,\\text{hour} & =60\\,\\text{minutes} \\\\  & =60\\cdot 1\\,\\text{minute} \\\\  & =60\\cdot 60\\,\\text{s} \\\\  & =3\\,600\\,\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3\\,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}} & =360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600}\\,\\frac{\\text{hour}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =(360\\cdot 1\\,000\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600})\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of the falcon is $100$ meters per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138442 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138442 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049689 #