 17592186045417] [17592186138592 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138592 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138592 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138592 #edb/ref 1045 "Converting Between Mixed Units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138592 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186139375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138592 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138592 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138592 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "89a1c0b6-a897-5db4-bd62-c855d5db6af2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186135916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52042" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138828 #edb/ref 1071 "Lewis spends $0.5$ hours per day studying. How many minutes per week does Lewis study for?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4e12086-1e27-5f26-877c-16c074270288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138828 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138828 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{hours}$ to $\\text{min}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hours}}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{day}$ into $\\text{week}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hours}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{week}}=\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{week}}=\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour.\n\n- There are $7$ days per week.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}} & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot 7\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{week}} \\\\  & =(0.5\\cdot 60\\cdot 7)\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hours}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{week}} \\\\  & =210\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, Lewis studies at a rate of $210\\,\\text{min/week}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139375 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139375 #edb/ref 1061 3049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139375 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23d7066f-ee02-59e9-ae85-3a1372e71eea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139375 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139376 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139376 #edb/ref 1061 3050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139376 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48400771-1498-544e-a6cb-80f32c6689d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139376 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139377 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139377 #edb/ref 1061 3053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139377 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16237576-82b8-5b51-891e-bf6a4b793259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139377 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139378 #edb/ref 1061 3056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139378 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "484759f2-e1e7-5618-9ef3-12349540dbce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139378 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139599 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use unit conversions to convert from one set of units to another.\n\nFor instance, the speed of sound is approximately $0.34$ kilometers per second. What is this in meters per second? We can find out using the following two steps.\n\n** Step 1: Create a conversion formula.**\n\nWe are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{s}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{km}$ with $\\textrm{m}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{km},$ we can put $\\textrm{km}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}} }{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{?}{\\cancel{ \\textrm{km} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThen, to introduce $\\textrm{m},$ we can put $\\textrm{m}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}} }{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m} }{\\cancel{ \\textrm{km} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m} }{\\textrm{km} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Use the conversion formula.**\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed that we wish to convert is $0.34 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{s}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.34\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}} & =0.34\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =(0.34\\cdot 1\\,000)\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =340\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of sound is $340$ meters per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139599 #edb/ref 1065 1404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139599 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d1f56c71-9cb3-5a5b-881c-bfbad588ca7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139599 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516128 #edb/ref 1080 "$6.9$ meters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516128 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516128 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "086d4688-33de-5a64-866f-902df4384ebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516155 #edb/ref 1080 "15\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516155 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516155 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15f65945-b500-528c-b740-17a557b9ff0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516168 #edb/ref 1080 "175\\,\\text{min/week}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516168 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516168 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e2988fa-d18c-5b90-ac1c-561062dc8688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516183 #edb/ref 1080 "0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516183 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516183 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2526bd24-622c-5111-ad94-fbb56ead3d23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516216 #edb/ref 1080 "600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516216 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516216 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "311a3504-e36b-5f10-892e-bdeb105186c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516255 #edb/ref 1080 "1\\,500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218651