186045417] [17592186516772 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516772 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516772 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516772 #edb/ref 1071 "A cruise ship on the high seas travels at a speed of $972$ centimeters per second. What is the speed of the ship in meters per second?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516772 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d475207-225c-5dcb-85b4-165f52b26314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516772 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{cm}$ with $\\text{m}.$ To get rid of $\\text{cm},$ we can put $\\text{cm}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed given in the problem statement is $972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters per meter. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters per centimeter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}} & =972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}} \\\\  & =972\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}) \\\\  & =9.72\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of the cruise is $9.72\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516803 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516803 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516803 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516803 #edb/ref 1071 "A racecar can reach a speed of $360$ kilometers per hour. What is this speed in kilometers per second?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516803 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5082eabf-27a1-5373-b336-872133f4c0e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516803 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{h}$ with $\\text{s}.$ To get rid of $\\text{h},$ we can put $\\text{h}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{s},$ we can put $\\text{s}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed given in the problem statement is $360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}.$\n\n- There are $3\\,600$ seconds per hour. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{3\\,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} & =360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600}\\,\\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =(360\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600})\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.1\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the race car reach a speed  of $0.1\\,\\text{km}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516809 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516809 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516809 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516809 #edb/ref 1071 "A car travels at a speed of $2.5$ kilometers per minute. What is this in kilometers per hour?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516809 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "642f1676-b974-5c20-b33e-f45029a01f5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516809 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{min}$ with $\\text{hour}.$ To get rid of $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{hour},$ we can put $\\text{hour}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes in one hour.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} & =2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}} \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 60(\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{hour}}) \\\\  & =150\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516830 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516830 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516830 #edb/ref 1071 "The acceleration of a cyclist is $1.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ What is the cyclist's acceleration in ${\\text{km/s}}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4020071-b095-5022-af4b-4598557e943b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516830 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{m}$ with $\\text{km}.$ To get rid of $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{km},$ we can put $\\text{km}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{m}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The acceleration given in the problem statement is $1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{1\\,000}$ kilometers per meter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}} & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}) \\\\  & =0.001\\,5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the acceleration of the cyclist is $0.001\\,5\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516861 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516861 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516861 #edb/ref 1071 "A tree sapling grows by $2$ centimeters per day. Assuming that one year has $365$ days, how many meters does the tree grow in one year?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e856700f-73de-59c0-8d3a-1f40df24d089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516861 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{cm}$ to $\\text{m}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{day}$ into $\\text{year}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{year}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{year}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters in one meter, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters in one centimeter.\n\n- There are $365$ days in one year.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}} & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}\\cdot 365\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{year}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot 365(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{year}}) \\\\  & =7.3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $2$ centimeters per day is equivalent to $7.3$ meters per year. In one year, the tree grows by $7.3$ meters." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
